    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper cites the original refusal-direction study but does not test or rely on the claim that one harmful-minus-harmless direction mediates refusal. Its own experiments steer broad multimodal concept dictionaries and learned SAE atoms, not the claimed single refusal direction.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports safety and utility outcomes for broad concept-based steering, plus over-refusal rates. These are related to the claim's subject but do not test its specified single direction or the V1–V8 claims. Hyperparameter plots and SAE reconstruction metrics are not extracted as claim-version results.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites the refusal-direction study (ref [4]) only among prior steering methods. It builds a broad WordNet/CC-3M concept dictionary and SAE atoms, then steers toward safety. It never isolates a harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, and never ablates it, adds it, or weight-edits with it. Its safety and over-refusal numbers measure its own detoxification method against baselines, not whether one direction mediates refusal.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is correct. The authors conclude DACO gives strong safety gains on MM-SafetyBench and JailBreakV-28K across QwenVL, LLaVA and InternVL, preserves general utility, adds modest overhead, keeps compliance on MOSSBench, and offers concept-level analysis.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper uses a broad multimodal concept dictionary and SAE-based compositional steering across selected layers and generated tokens, rather than isolating and ablating or adding one harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports that safety changes with the SAE annotation threshold η and positive-concept strength γ; raising γ improves safety but reduces utility, while overly large η harms safety and utility. The reported intervention uses layers 15–19 and the first 50 generated tokens.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "SAE-based compositional concept steering (DACO) raises safety-judge scores substantially on MM-SafetyBench and JailBreakV-28K across three 7–8B MLLMs, with small utility losses. For example, Qwen2.5-VL MS-R rises from 0.442 to 0.990 while MMMU falls from 0.546 to 0.521.",
        "proposed_claim": "Broad concept-dictionary/SAE activation steering improves multimodal safety-benchmark scores while largely preserving utility; this says nothing about single-direction refusal mediation.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
